# Without this, `COPY . .` drags the HOST's node_modules into the image and
# overwrites the Linux dependency tree installed inside it — pnpm's symlink
# farm and any native binary become wrong for the container's platform, and
# the build fails with a confusing "cannot find module" on a package that is
# obviously installed.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output — regenerated inside the image.
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
out
**/out
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Local state and secrets. `.env.example` is intentionally NOT ignored.
#
# The `**/` prefix is NOT decoration. A bare `.env` pattern matches ONLY at the
# build-context root — `apps/web/.env.local` sails straight past it and into the
# image, where Next.js then auto-loads it at runtime. That exact bug shipped a
# live bot token inside a locally built image and silently overrode the
# container's rate-limit config. Every secret pattern below must be `**/`-rooted,
# and ci.yml asserts the result by scanning the built image.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/.dev.vars
.wrangler
**/.wrangler

# Test artefacts
coverage
**/coverage
playwright-report
**/playwright-report
test-results
**/test-results

# VCS and editor noise
.git
.github
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

# Docs do not affect the build.
*.md
docs

# ── Repo tooling that has no business in a runtime image ──────────────
#
# `.gitleaks.toml` is the reason this section exists, and the failure it
# caused is worth writing down because it was two security controls
# tripping over each other rather than a leak.
#
# ci.yml plants a decoy secret, builds the image, and greps the filesystem
# for the decoy's literal value — a real end-to-end proof that
# `.dockerignore` is working. `.gitleaks.toml` ALLOWLISTS that same literal,
# so gitleaks does not flag the planted decoy. `COPY . .` then carried the
# allowlist into the image, the grep found its own decoy string inside the
# scanner's config, and the gate failed pointing at an image that was
# genuinely clean.
#
# The fix is not to narrow the grep. It is that none of these files are
# part of the application: they configure checks that run in CI, on a
# checkout, and shipping them only widens what an attacker who lands a
# shell can read about how this project defends itself.
.gitleaks.toml
.gitleaksignore
.husky
lighthouserc*
codecov.yml
renovate.json

# NOT excluded, deliberately: eslint.config.mjs, .prettierrc and friends.
# r12 made the production build's ESLint pass actually run, so the config
# is a BUILD input now — dropping it here would break `next build` inside
# the image, and the error would point at lint rather than at this file.
